---
// Recognition section for the heal-the-healer door — the space holder's own
// state, named the way they would say it themselves: what the work leaves in
// the body, why knowing the words for it changes nothing, and why the method
// has to begin in their own body before it can become anything for a client.
// The accent quote is Jacob's own (copy book §4 / quote 3); the pull line is
// the reframe "not letting go — letting in" (§8). The ad creative for this
// campaign says "release"; the page says what the practice actually does —
// acknowledgment (copy book law 2) — so nobody arrives expecting a valve.
//
// The audience is international — many read English as a second language.
// Plain international English: complete sentences, common words, no idioms.
---

<section class="we-opening">
  <div class="we-opening-inner">
    <div class="we-eyebrow">You know this feeling</div>
    <h2 class="we-opening-head">
      You hold space for everyone.<br />
      Nobody asks <em>what it leaves in you.</em>
    </h2>
    <p class="we-opening-body">
      All week you sit with people in their most difficult moments. You keep
      your own nervous system steady so that theirs can settle. You hear
      things that are not easy to hear — and then you drive home. Something
      of every one of those hours stays in your body. Not as a story you
      could tell at dinner: as a weight in the chest, a tightness in the
      throat, a tiredness that a weekend doesn't reach.
    </p>
    <p class="we-opening-body">
      You know the words for this — compassion fatigue, secondary stress,
      burnout — and knowing them changes nothing. That is the honest part:
      your training taught you how to hold what another person brings. It
      never taught you where to put it afterwards. Most people who hold space
      for a living have no practice of their own where any of it is ever
      heard.
    </p>
    <p class="we-opening-body">
      Here is why it stays. What arrived through the body wants to be met in
      the body. Insight doesn't reach it — you can know exactly whose grief
      you are carrying and still be carrying it. And the usual advice,
      <em>let it go</em>, asks you to drop something you never actually
      picked up and looked at. A feeling that was never given expression
      stays exactly where it is.
    </p>
    <p class="we-opening-body">
      Somatic Vocal Healing meets it at the level where it lives. One breath
      in, one honest tone out — the sound <em>of</em> what's actually there.
      Not singing. Not screaming. Not a technique you perform on yourself.
      The sound of the weight, and then the three seconds of quiet after it,
      where the body registers that it was heard. It takes thirty seconds. It
      can't be done wrong. And it was yours before you had words.
    </p>
    <p class="we-opening-body">
      This matters for your work as much as for you. A settled nervous system
      beside someone is what helps theirs settle — you already know that. You
      cannot hold for others what you have never let work on you. So this
      masterclass begins where the whole method begins: in your own body. You
      come as a professional. For a hundred minutes, you are the one the
      space is held for.
    </p>
    <p class="we-opening-accent">
      "Your voice is an ally of the nervous system. If you don't use your
      voice, the nervous system can stay dysregulated." — Jacob
    </p>
    <p class="we-opening-pull">
      Not letting go —<br />letting in.
    </p>
    <a href="#register" class="we-link-cta">
      Reserve your seat <i class="ph-light ph-arrow-right"></i>
    </a>
  </div>
</section>
